\citet{Humphreys:2019:L26} reported the detection of VO in the circumstellar material of VY Canis Majoris using Hubble Space Telescope observations.  They identify bandheads of the B$^4\Pi$--X$^4\Sigma$ electronic transition around 7900\AA {and 8600\AA} based on the laboratory work of \citet{Cheung:1994:443} and \citet{Adam:1995:94}.

    %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
    %%%%%%%  THREE ATOM MOLECULES %%%%%%
    %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

    \begin{center}
        \large
        \textsc{\textbf{Three-Atom Molecules}}
        \normalsize
    \end{center}

The detection of NCS in Yebes 40-m observations of TMC-1 was reported by \citet{Cernicharo:2021:L3}.  Three hyperfine-split components of the $J = 5/2\rightarrow3/2$ transition of NCS at 42.7\,GHz were identified and assigned based on the laboratory work of \citet{Amano:1991:2275}, \citet{McCarthy:2003:287}, and \citet{Maeda:2007:477}.

    %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
    %%%%%%%  FOUR ATOM MOLECULES %%%%%%%
    %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

    \begin{center}
        \large
        \textsc{\textbf{Four-Atom Molecules}}
        \normalsize
    \end{center}

The detection of HCCS was reported in Yebes 40-m observations of TMC-1 by \citet{Cernicharo:2021:L3}.  They identify and assign the $F = 4 \rightarrow 3$ and $F = 3 \rightarrow 2$ hyperfine components of the $J = 7/2\rightarrow5/2$ transition of HCCS at 41.1\,GHz based on the laboratory work of \citet{Kim:2002:83} and \citet{Vrtilek:1992:L73}.


    %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
    %%%%%%%  FIVE ATOM MOLECULES %%%%%%%
    %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

    \begin{center}
        \large
        \textsc{\textbf{Five-Atom Molecules}}
        \normalsize
    \end{center}

\citet{Rodriguez-Almeida:2021:L11} reported the detection of CHOSH in IRAM 30-m and Yebes 40-m observations of G+0.693-0.027.  Based on the laboratory work of \citet{Hocking:1976:995}, they identify and assign nine largely unblended $a$-type transitions between 34.2--93.5\,GHz.	

    %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
    %%%%%%%%  SIX ATOM MOLECULES %%%%%%%
    %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%	

    \begin{center}
        \large
        \textsc{\textbf{Six-Atom Molecules}}
        \normalsize
    \end{center}

The detection of \ce{CH2CCH} was reported by \citet{Agundez:2021:L10} in Yebes 40-m observations of TMC-1.  Based on the laboratory work of \citet{Tanaka:1997:2728}, they identify and assigned six hyperfine components of the $2_{0,2} - 1_{0,1}$ transition near 37.5\,GHz.

    %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
    %%%%%%%  SEVEN ATOM MOLECULES %%%%%%
    %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%	

    \begin{center}
        \large
        \textsc{\textbf{Seven-Atom Molecules}}
        \normalsize
    \end{center}

\citet{Cernicharo:2021:L15a} reported the detection of $c$-\ce{C3HCCH} in Yebes 40-m observations of TMC-1.  Based on the laboratory work of \citet{Travers:1997:L135} they identify and assign ten largely unblended transitions, and a number of others, between 31.5--44.0\,GHz to $c$-\ce{C3HCCH}.

    %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
    %%%%%%%  EIGHT ATOM MOLECULES %%%%%%
    %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%	

    \begin{center}
        \large
        \textsc{\textbf{Eight-Atom Molecules}}
        \normalsize
    \end{center}

\citet{Cernicharo:2021:L2} reported the detection of \ce{CH2CHCCH} in Yebes 40-m observations of TMC-1.  {They identify and assign seven transitions between 35.5--46.4\,GHz} based on the laboratory work of \citet{Sobolev:1961:78}, \citet{Thorwirth:2003:L11}, and \citet{Thorwirth:2004:263}.

    %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
    %%%%%%%  NINE ATOM MOLECULES %%%%%%%
    %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%	

    \begin{center}
        \large
        \textsc{\textbf{Nine-Atom Molecules}}
        \normalsize
    \end{center}

\citet{Lee:2021:L11} reported the detection of \ce{H2CCHC3N} in GBT 100-m observations of TMC-1.  Using spectral line stacking and matched filtering techniques, they reported a 5.5$\sigma$ significance detection based on the laboratory work of \citet{Halter:2001:12353}, \citet{Thorwirth:2004:93}, and \citet{McCarthy:2020:5170}.

    %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
    %%%%%%%%  TEN ATOM MOLECULES %%%%%%%
    %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%	

    \begin{center}
        \large
        \textsc{\textbf{Ten-Atom Molecules}}
        \normalsize
    \end{center}

The detection of \ce{CH3OCH2OH} was reported by \citet{McGuire:2017:L46} in ALMA observations of NGC 6334I.  More than two dozen largely unblended transitions between 239--349~GHz were identified and assigned based on the laboratory work of \citet{Motiyenko:2018:5509}, which was in press at the time.

    %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
    %%%%%%  ELEVEN ATOM MOLECULES %%%%%%
    %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

    \begin{center}
        \large
        \textsc{\textbf{Eleven-Atom Molecules}}
        \normalsize
    \end{center}

The detection of \ce{C5H6} in Yebes 40-m observations of TMC-1 was reported by \citet{Cernicharo:2021:L15a}.  Based on the laboratory work of \citet{Laurie:1956:635}, \citet{Scharpen:1965:2765}, \citet{Flygare:1970:7523}, and \citet{Bogey:1988:277}, they identify and assign six largely unblended transitions of \ce{C5H6} between 37.5--46.8\,GHz.

    %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
    %%%%%%  TWELVE ATOM MOLECULES %%%%%%
    %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

    \begin{center}
        \large
        \textsc{\textbf{Twelve-Atom Molecules}}
        \normalsize
    \end{center}

The detection of 2-\ce{C5H5CN} was reported by \citet{Lee:2021:L2a} using GBT 100-m observations of TMC-1.  Based on laboratory work presented in the same paper, as well as work from \citet{Ford:1978:326}, \citet{Sakaizumi:1987:3903}, and \citet{McCarthy:2021:176}, they report a 6.6$\sigma$ detection based on spectral line stacking and matched filtering.

    %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
    %%%%%  THIRTEEN ATOM MOLECULES %%%%%
    %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

    \begin{center}
        \large
        \textsc{\textbf{Thirteen-Atom Molecules}}
        \normalsize
    \end{center}

\citet{Loomis:2021:188a} reported the detection of \ce{HC11N} at 5.0$\sigma$ using spectral line stacking and matching filtering analyses of GBT 100-m observations of TMC-1.  The detection was based on the laboratory work of \citet{Travers:1996:L65}.

    The study of \ce{HC11N} in the ISM has a long history.  \citet{Bell:1997:L61} reported a detection of \ce{HC11N} in NRAO 140-foot observations of TMC-1.  They assigned two emission features at 12848.728~MHz and 13186.853~MHz to the $J = 38\rightarrow37$ and $39\rightarrow38$ transitions of \ce{HC11N} based on the laboratory work of \citet{Travers:1996:L65}.  The detection was later disputed by \citet{Loomis:2016:4175} who re-observed TMC-1 using the 100-m GBT.  They targeted six transitions of \ce{HC11N}, including those originally reported by \citet{Bell:1997:L61}, and detected no signal at any of these frequencies.  They suggest correlator artifacts may have been responsible for the signals seen in \citet{Bell:1997:L61}.  Subsequently, \citet{Cordiner:2017:187} confirmed the findings of \citet{Loomis:2016:4175}, setting a yet lower upper limit with more sensitive data.	

    %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
    %%%%%  PAHs %%%%%
    %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

    \begin{center}
        \large
        \textsc{\textbf{Polycyclic Aromatic Hydrocarbon Molecules}}
        \normalsize
    \end{center}

The detection of \ce{C9H8} was reported nearly simultaneously by \citet{Burkhardt:2021:L18a} in GBT 100-m observations of TMC-1 and \citet{Cernicharo:2021:L15a} in Yebes 40-m observations of TMC-1.  Both detections relied on the laboratory work of \citet{Li:1979:171}, with \citet{Burkhardt:2021:L18a} presenting new laboratory spectroscopy of \ce{C9H8} as well.	

